                    {
                        "sense_id": "7f0bec3b-de88-3bc1-81fc-6ea970bbaec7",
                        "entry_id": "146db8c2-ed77-3568-9204-60a7952e91f8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A male given name from Greek, notably used as a stock name for a shepherd in pastorals, in particular a shepherd whose love for a boy is described in Virgil's Eclogues."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "literary"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c61b8e5a-5509-4e30-9f02-090f28053c03",
                                "sense_id": "7f0bec3b-de88-3bc1-81fc-6ea970bbaec7",
                                "text": "They give voice to Corydon′s passion; they confront in their own culture the constraints that hedge his passion in."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cd9e497c-eab1-4a30-b3cf-b0810bf90f8b",
                                "sense_id": "7f0bec3b-de88-3bc1-81fc-6ea970bbaec7",
                                "text": "Byron shocked regency England by citing it^([Virgil's second eclogue]) in the opening canto of Don Juan, and Andre Gide gave the title Corydon to the controversial defense of homosexuality he published in 1924.\nVirgil depicts Corydon’s passion with psychological realism and a touch of wry irony."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d3d51ae2-ffad-4efd-94cb-2a717635dc1a",
                                "sense_id": "7f0bec3b-de88-3bc1-81fc-6ea970bbaec7",
                                "text": "The harlequinade ceases for the moment, and a two-character tragedy begins with the shepherds Thyrsis and Corydon, whose names derive from ancient pastoral poetry."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
